# Scenario

**Organization:** CloudNative (fictional) — same cloud-native SaaS company as
Project 02.
**Objective:** Formally document risks the business has chosen to accept rather
than remediate — and make the case for each acceptance in language a business
owner, not a security person, would sign off on.

## Why risk acceptance is its own skill

Most GRC portfolios stop at identifying a risk. The harder, more senior part of
the job is what happens after: not every risk gets remediated on the timeline
security would prefer, and the job is to make sure that when a business decides
to accept a risk, the decision is documented, owned by the right person, bounded
by a review date, and backed by something that actually reduces exposure in the
meantime — not just a shrug and an email thread nobody can find six months later.

## Three risks, three different reasons to accept

I documented three risk acceptances for CloudNative, each testing a different
kind of judgment call:

1. **RA-001 — Legacy finance tool without MFA.** A cost-vs-exposure call: the
   fix (migrate platforms, or build a custom auth wrapper) costs more than the
   actual exposure justifies for a 3-person, VPN-restricted tool. This is the
   one I built out as a full worked example — see `deliverables/RA-001-Legacy-
   Finance-Admin-MFA.docx` — because it's the version of this document a
   non-technical business owner actually has to read and sign, and getting that
   tone right (plain language, no jargon, a clear ask) is the actual point of
   the exercise.
2. **RA-002 — Vendor without SOC 2 Type II.** A business-necessity call: the
   tool is needed for a Q4 deadline, no certified alternative fits the
   timeline, and the compensating control is scope-limiting what data the
   vendor ever touches (no PII, aggregated metrics only) rather than waiting
   on a certification with no committed date.
3. **RA-003 — Known vulnerability on a system being retired.** A time-bound
   call: patching effort isn't worth it for a system with a fixed 90-day
   remaining lifespan, so the acceptance is explicitly tied to the
   decommission date rather than left open-ended.

## What I'd push back on if I were reviewing this, not writing it

RA-001 is the strongest of the three because the review trigger is genuinely
enforceable (a vendor feature release or a fixed date). RA-002's trigger — 
contract renewal, over a year out — is the weakest: a lot can change in a
vendor's security posture in a year without anyone revisiting this. In a real
engagement I'd push for a 90-day interim check-in on RA-002, not just a
renewal-date review.
